     "type": "example",
     "title": "What the Meta-Analyses Actually Found",
     "content": "When researchers pooled results across many individual studies, a more modest and more specific picture of emotional intelligence emerged than the popular narrative suggested. Meta-analyses of trait EI measures consistently find strong overlap with existing personality dimensions - correlations with low neuroticism often around negative zero-point-five and with extraversion often around zero-point-four are typical figures reported in these reviews - which is exactly the overlap critics point to when arguing that trait EI may be substantially old wine in a new bottle. Ability EI, measured through performance tests like the MSCEIT, tends to correlate more modestly with those same personality traits, which supports the claim that it is measuring something at least somewhat distinct, but its correlation with concrete outcomes such as job performance is typically smaller than popular accounts implied, often in a range that would be described as small-to-moderate by conventional standards, and it frequently shrinks further once general cognitive ability and personality are statistically controlled for. General cognitive ability, by contrast, has one of the most extensively replicated and comparatively larger relationships with job performance across occupations of almost any individual-difference variable psychology has studied. None of this means emotional intelligence is worthless as a research construct; it means the specific, falsifiable claims that survive careful measurement are narrower and more qualified than the sweeping public framing that made the original book a phenomenon. The gap between a compelling narrative built around vivid anecdotes and case studies, and a hedged statistical finding buried in a supplementary table, is itself one of the central lessons of the critical reception.",
